coder › Done. I implemented `parseIni` in `src/ini.ts` and added tests in `src/ini.test.ts`, committed as `e258bc0`.

## What I changed

**`src/ini.ts`** — replaced the stub with a real parser:
- Splits the input on `\r?\n` and trims each line.
- Skips blank lines and lines starting with `;` or `#` (after trimming, so indented comments count).
- A line starting with `[` and ending with `]` is a section header; the section name is the content between, trimmed.
- Otherwise the first `=` splits key from value; the key and value are trimmed. If the trimmed value starts and ends with a double quote, it is unwrapped, keeping the interior (leading/trailing spaces, `;`, `#`) verbatim. Missing values become `''`.
- Keys land under the current section (`''` until a header appears). Sections and `''` are created lazily, so empty/comment-only input returns `{}` rather than a phantom empty section. Later keys overwrite earlier ones in the same section (including when a section header repeats).
- A line that is none of the above (no `=`) throws `SyntaxError` with a message naming the 1-based line number, e.g. `line 3: expected "key = value"`.

**`src/ini.test.ts`** — added 16 tests alongside the existing one, covering: the empty-string section, whitespace trimming, comments/blank lines, quoted values preserving inner spaces and `;`/`#`, quoted empty value, unquoted `;` preserved, first-`=` splitting (`k=b=c`), missing values, override within a section, repeated sections, multiple sections, CRLF endings, empty/comment-only input, and `SyntaxError` messages with correct 1-based line numbers (including counting preceding comments/blank lines).

## Checks

`bun test` — **17 pass, 0 fail** (20 `expect()` calls).

## Decisions the plan didn't spell out

- Sections are only recognized when the trimmed line both starts with `[` and ends with `]`; a header missing its closing bracket falls through to the key/value rule and (lacking `=`) throws a line-numbered `SyntaxError`.
- Comment markers only count when the line *starts* with them, so `;` inside an unquoted value is preserved.
- Quotes are only stripped when the whole trimmed value is wrapped (`"..."`); a value like `"a"b` is kept literally.
- Empty/comment-only input returns `{}`; the `''` section exists only if a pre-header key actually appears.

